## 117. Pattern Signature Example

Words:

```text
APPLE
```

Pattern:

```text
A P P L E
1 2 2 3 4
```

Word:

```text
HELLO
```

Pattern:

```text
H E L L O
1 2 3 3 4
```

Therefore they have different repetition structures.

---

## 118. Symbol Pattern Coding

If:

```text
A = ○
B = △
C = □
```

then:

```text
ABC → ○△□
```

But if:

```text
same letters = same symbols
```

then:

```text
BANANA
```

must have the same repeated-symbol pattern:

```text
X Y Z Y Z Y
```

This is a useful consistency check.

---

## 119. Frequency Table

For any word, build:

| Letter | Frequency |
|---|---:|
| A | 1 |
| P | 2 |
| L | 1 |
| E | 1 |

This can reveal hidden numerical codes.

---

## 120. Position Parity Coding

For each letter:

```text
odd alphabet position → O
even alphabet position → E
```

Example:

```text
CODE
```

```text
C=3 → O
O=15 → O
D=4 → E
E=5 → O
```

Result:

```text
O O E O
```

Could be represented as:

```text
1 1 0 1
```

---

## 121. Vowel Position Coding

For:

```text
EDUCATION
```

mark:

```text
E = V
D = C
U = V
C = C
A = V
T = C
I = V
O = V
N = C
```

Pattern:

```text
V C V C V C V V C
```

This can be encoded as:

```text
1 0 1 0 1 0 1 1 0
```

---

## 122. Mixed Binary Coding

A word can be represented by multiple binary properties.

For example:

```text
vowel = 1
consonant = 0
```

and separately:

```text
odd position = 1
even position = 0
```

So each letter can have a pair:

```text
A = vowel + odd
  = 11

B = consonant + even
  = 00
```

This creates a 2-bit signature.

---

## 123. Coding as a Function

Mathematically, coding is a function:

```text
f(input) = code
```

Example:

```text
f(x) = x + 1
```

for alphabet positions.

Then:

```text
f(A) = B
f(B) = C
...
f(Z) = A
```

This is a cyclic function.

---

## 124. Inverse Coding

Some questions give the code and ask for the original.

If:

```text
Original + 2 = Code
```

then:

```text
Original = Code - 2
```

Example:

```text
E → G
```

So:

```text
G → E
```

when decoding.

---

## 125. Encoding vs Decoding

```text
ENCODING:
Original → Code

DECODING:
Code → Original
```

Diagram:

```text
        ENCODE
A B C ─────────→ B C D

        DECODE
B C D ─────────→ A B C
```

Always identify the direction of the question.

---
